# AGENTS.md

This repo is the process registry. It holds process definitions only.
Run output lives in the run folder. QA criteria live in a separate repo, `{{qa_name}}`.

## Local settings

- Run folder root: `{{runs_root}}`
- QA repo: `{{qa_name}}` (makers have no access, by design)
- Record of work: {{record}}
- Default bounce cap: 3

## Map

| Path | What it is |
|---|---|
| `ROUTE.md` | Which runbook fits which request |
| `runbooks/<id>/SKILL.md` | Entry point for one process |
| `runbooks/<id>/process.yaml` | Owner, version, status, stage order |
| `runbooks/<id>/NN-name/STAGE.md` | Instructions for one stage |
| `runbooks/<id>/NN-name/deliverable.md` | What that stage must hand in |
| `runbooks/<id>/NN.1-qa/GATE.md` | A QA gate. Handoff only, no criteria |
| `skills/<id>/SKILL.md` | Shared skills that stages can use |
| `tools/` | lint, scaffold, graph, sync |

## Load only what you need

Read down these layers and stop as soon as you can act.

1. `ROUTE.md`
2. The runbook's `SKILL.md` and `process.yaml`
3. `STAGE.md` and `deliverable.md` for the stage you are working
4. The files that `STAGE.md` lists under Inputs

Less context gives better work. Do not read other stages or other runbooks for background.

## 1. Route

Find the row in `ROUTE.md` whose "Use when" matches the request.

- One match: open that runbook.
- No match, or more than one: stop. Say what was asked and why nothing fits. Record it as a task for the registry steward. Do not guess and do not invent a process.

## 2. Run

`process.yaml` is the source of truth for order.

1. Start a run folder at `{{runs_root}}/<runbook-id>/<run-id>/`. Write `run.yaml` with the registry commit, the start time and the harness.
2. A stage is ready when every stage in its `needs` list is complete.
3. A stage is complete when its output is handed in and, if it has a gate, the gate has passed.
4. Start every ready stage. Stages that share a number (`02a`, `02b`) are built to run at the same time. Give each its own task and its own output folder.
5. A stage listed in `human_gate` waits for a named human to say yes on the task record before it starts.
6. The run is done when the last stage is complete.

One stage is one job with one task record. Never fold two stages into one job.

## 3. If you are the maker of a stage

- Read `STAGE.md` and `deliverable.md`, then the inputs they name. Nothing else.
- Take inputs only from earlier stages' `output/` folders. Never from their working files.
- Write your deliverable to `<run folder>/<stage-id>/output/`.
- You are done when `deliverable.md` is satisfied. Then hand off. If the stage has a gate, mark the task for review and pass it to the QA agent.
- You will not see how the deliverable is tested. That is deliberate. Aim at the outcome in `deliverable.md`, not at a test.
- Do not open `{{qa_name}}`. Do not edit any `GATE.md`.

## 4. QA gates

A folder named `NN.1-qa` after a stage means that stage has a gate. No folder means no gate.

- A different agent grades. Never the maker.
- The grader's rules and criteria are in `{{qa_name}}`, at the same path as the gate folder.
- The grader reads your `output/` folder only.

## 5. Rework

A failed gate returns a note that starts `QA REVIEW: FIX n/<cap>`.

- Fix every numbered item under **Fix**.
- Leave everything under **Keep** alone.
- Hand in again. The grader checks the whole deliverable again, not just your fixes.
- At the cap, the task goes to the registry steward as blocked. Repeated fails usually mean the instructions or the spec are wrong, so the steward fixes the process itself.

## 6. Receipts

Every finished stage leaves a receipt on the task record with:

- the runbook id and stage id
- `process@<registry commit>`
- for gated stages, `qa@<QA repo commit>`, added by the grader
- where the output is

## 7. Changing a process

- Change through a pull request. Never edit the main branch directly.
- Bump `version` in `process.yaml` when the steps or the deliverable change.
- The maker of a stage never writes its test. Criteria change only in `{{qa_name}}`, by the steward.
- Run `python tools/lint.py . --qa <path to {{qa_name}}>` before opening the pull request. Without QA access, run it without `--qa`.
- Add `--against <checkout of the main branch>` to list every change that could lower the bar: a removed gate, a changed `human_gate`, a promoted status, a raised bounce cap, or new instructions with no version bump. Put that list in the pull request. A person looks at each one.
- Never make lint pass by weakening the process. Removing a gate, setting `human_gate` to `[]` or claiming a status to clear a message is a failure, not a fix.
- To add or adopt a process, use the `registry-steward` runbook.

## Status ladder

`draft` → `verified` (gates pass on one harness) → `portable` (two or more harnesses) → `retired`.
An existing process enters as `adopted`: captured as it runs today, not yet tested.
